<!-- §01 -->
<section id="s-spec" data-code="spec">
<div class="section-no">01 — Speculative Decoding</div>
<h2>Why Every Token Costs the Same</h2>
<p>A 700-billion-parameter language model does the same amount of computation whether it outputs the word "the" or a breakthrough mathematical insight. Each token requires one full forward pass through all layers — there's no shortcut for easy tokens. At 400GB of weights, even a single token takes milliseconds.</p>
<p>Speculative decoding flips this equation. Instead of paying the full price per token, it lets a small, cheap <strong>draft model</strong> guess the next K tokens all at once. Then the big model verifies all K in a <em>single</em> forward pass — which is nearly free since attention sees them together. Any token that matches the big model's distribution is accepted; the first mismatch drops the tail.</p>
<p>The result: if the draft is good enough that K=3 tokens get accepted on average, you've done 3 tokens' worth of work in roughly 1 forward pass. The key metric is <strong>accept length τ</strong> — the average number of tokens accepted per verification step.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speculative_decode.py — one round of speculation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click steps</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="spec-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" data-step="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Generate K draft tokens</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The small draft model autoregressively produces K candidate tokens — fast, cheap, O(K) sequential passes through a tiny network.</div>
          <div class="step-code">drafts = draft_model.generate("The cat sat", K=4)<br>→ [" on", " the", " mat", "."]</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Single-pass verification</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The big target model processes the original context + all K drafts in ONE forward pass. This is the key insight — parallel attention is nearly free vs sequential generation.</div>
          <div class="step-code">logits = target_model("The cat sat on the mat.", batch=True)<br>→ 5 positions verified at once</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Accept or reject each position</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Compare draft probability q(x) vs target probability p(x) at each position. Accept if p/q ≥ uniform sample u ~ U[0,1]. Reject at first mismatch and resample from corrected distribution.</div>
          <div class="step-code">for i, (p, q) in enumerate(zip(target_probs, draft_probs)):<br>    if rand() &lt; p[x_i] / q[x_i]: accept<br>    else: resample and break</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Accepted tokens = free speedup</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Every accepted draft token was verified in the batch pass — it's essentially free. Speedup ≈ τ / (1 + γ·c) where γ is overhead and c is cost ratio of draft/target.</div>
          <div class="step-code">speedup = tau / (1 + gamma * cost_ratio)<br># EAGLE-3: tau=6.62 → speedup ~5.5×<br># MTP (GLM-5): tau=2.76 → speedup ~2×</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="spec-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="spec-prog" style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="spec-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The draft model is everything. An ideal drafter is fast, cheap, and highly correlated with the big model's distribution. This is where the three major approaches diverge sharply.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 -->
<section id="s-three" data-code="three">
<div class="section-no">02 — Three Drafting Approaches</div>
<h2>External Neural, Diffusion Block, or Internal MTP</h2>
<p>The critical architectural decision is <em>where</em> the draft tokens come from. Three major approaches have emerged, each with fundamentally different trade-offs between acceptance rate, integration depth, and deployment complexity.</p>

<div class="approach-grid">
  <div class="approach-card">
    <span class="approach-tag tag-ext">External</span>
    <h4 style="color:var(--cyan)">EAGLE-3</h4>
    <p>A separate lightweight transformer trained on the top-layer hidden states of the target model. Learns to mimic the target's next-token distribution using feature-level supervision. Achieves τ ≈ 6.6 on Vicuna 13B — highest acceptance of any approach.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.5rem;color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--text2)">Trade-off:</strong> Requires training a new drafter per base model. Can't be integrated at pretraining time.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="approach-card">
    <span class="approach-tag tag-diff">Diffusion</span>
    <h4 style="color:var(--amber)">DFlash (MiMo)</h4>
    <p>Block-diffusion speculation: treats a window of K future tokens as a joint diffusion process, iteratively denoising them in parallel. Used by Xiaomi's MiMo to break 1000 tokens/second on a 1T-parameter MoE, combined with MXFP4 quantization.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.5rem;color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--text2)">Trade-off:</strong> Powerful for large batches; requires diffusion-aware training. Less plug-and-play.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="approach-card highlight">
    <span class="approach-tag tag-int">Internal</span>
    <h4 style="color:var(--purple-l)">MTP (GLM-5) ★</h4>
    <p>Extra transformer layers appended to the main model during pretraining, each predicting one additional future token. No separate drafter — the draft is baked into the base model weights. GLM-5's parameter-sharing variant achieves τ = 2.76.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.5rem;color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--text2)">Trade-off:</strong> Lower raw τ than EAGLE-3, but zero marginal deployment cost — just use the model you already have.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The contrast is stark: EAGLE-3 gets the best acceptance rate but requires a whole separate model; MTP gets a lower but still useful rate with nothing extra to deploy. DFlash occupies a middle ground suited for massive-scale inference clusters.</p>
<p>For frontier labs deploying 700B+ models where every additional component adds operational complexity, MTP's <strong>zero-overhead integration</strong> is often worth accepting a lower τ. The real question becomes: can MTP's acceptance rate be pushed higher without increasing memory?</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 -->
<section id="s-memory" data-code="memory">
<div class="section-no">03 — MTP's Memory Dilemma</div>
<h2>N Speculative Steps → N Extra Transformer Layers</h2>
<p>Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) works by training the model to predict not just the next token but the next N tokens simultaneously. Each prediction depth gets its own transformer layer — a small but complete transformer block that takes the main model's hidden state and predicts one more step ahead.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">MTP architecture</div>
<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t</sub> = main_transformer(x<sub>1..t</sub>)  <span class="math-comment">← main model hidden state</span><br>
<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+1</sub> = output_head(<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t</sub>)               <span class="math-comment">← next token (standard)</span><br>
<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+2</sub> = mtp₁(norm(<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t</sub>), emb(<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+1</sub>))  <span class="math-comment">← depth-1 MTP layer</span><br>
<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+3</sub> = mtp₂(norm(<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t+1</sub>), emb(<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+2</sub>)) <span class="math-comment">← depth-2 MTP layer</span><br>
<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+4</sub> = mtp₃(norm(<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t+2</sub>), emb(<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+3</sub>)) <span class="math-comment">← depth-3 MTP layer</span>
</div>

<p>The problem: to speculate K tokens ahead during inference, you need K separate MTP layers at training time. Each layer has its own weights and its own KV cache during generation. For a 744B parameter model trained with 4 speculative steps, the MTP module alone can add tens of gigabytes of weights and cache — scaling <em>linearly</em> with K.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">MTP memory cost vs speculative steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">observe the linear growth</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="min-width:580px">
    <canvas id="memory-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>DeepSeek-V3 found a practical compromise: train with a <em>single</em> MTP layer, then at inference time reuse it to speculate 2 tokens (depth 1 and depth 2) through the same layer twice. Memory stays flat — only 1 set of MTP weights — but now there's a <strong>training-inference mismatch</strong>: the model was trained to predict depth-1 tokens but at inference is asked to also predict depth-2 tokens through the same weights. The second-token acceptance rate suffers.</p>

<p>The fundamental tension: more depth = better acceptance, more depth = more memory. Can you get one without the other?</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 -->
<section id="s-sharing" data-code="sharing">
<div class="section-no">04 — Parameter Sharing: The GLM-5 Trick</div>
<h2>Three Depths, One Set of Weights</h2>
<p>GLM-5's insight is deceptively simple: <strong>train 3 MTP layers but share their parameters</strong>. All three layers use the same weights — the same attention matrices, the same FFN projection weights. At each training step, the same layer is unrolled 3 times, each time predicting one step further ahead.</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Parameter-shared MTP (GLM-5)</div>
<span class="math-comment">Same MTP weights W used at all depths</span><br>
<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+2</sub> = W(norm(<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t</sub>),   emb(<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+1</sub>)) <span class="math-comment">← depth 1: predict token 2</span><br>
<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+3</sub> = W(norm(<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t+1</sub>), emb(<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+2</sub>)) <span class="math-comment">← depth 2: predict token 3</span><br>
<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+4</sub> = W(norm(<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t+2</sub>), emb(<span class="math-var">ŷ</span><sub>t+3</sub>)) <span class="math-comment">← depth 3: predict token 4</span><br>
<span class="math-comment">Memory = 1 MTP layer (same as DeepSeek-V3)</span>
</div>

<p>Why does this help? Because the model <em>trains</em> at all three depths. When gradient flows back through depth 1, depth 2, and depth 3 simultaneously, the weights learn to be useful across all prediction distances — not just depth 1. At inference, when the same weights are used to speculate depth-2 and depth-3 tokens, they've been explicitly trained for those positions. The training-inference gap closes.</p>

<div class="vp">
