  </div>
</div>

<p>The key intuition: with K=1, the model only practices predicting position t+2. When you ask it to predict t+3 and t+4 at inference, it's improvising. With K=3 shared layers, the model has explicitly trained on positions t+2, t+3, and t+4 — using the same weights for all three, which acts as a regularizer forcing the weights to generalize across depths.</p>

<p>Compare this to training 3 <em>separate</em> MTP layers (as vanilla MTP would do): you'd get better acceptance than parameter sharing, but 3× the memory cost. Parameter sharing is the sweet spot: it matches the <em>training coverage</em> of 3 separate layers at the <em>memory cost</em> of 1 layer.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 -->
<section id="s-compare" data-code="compare">
<div class="section-no">05 — Comparative Landscape</div>
<h2>Trade-offs Across All Three Approaches</h2>
<p>Each drafting strategy occupies a distinct region of the design space. The axes that matter most for deployment are: <strong>acceptance rate τ</strong> (higher = faster), <strong>deployment overhead</strong> (does it require extra models, training, or serving infrastructure?), and <strong>integration depth</strong> (is it baked into the base model or layered on top?).</p>

