<p>EAGLE-3's τ ≈ 6.6 dwarfs MTP's 2.76 — but that number is on Vicuna 13B with a purpose-trained drafter. On a 744B production model at scale, running a separate drafter adds KV cache, increases memory bandwidth pressure, and creates synchronization overhead between the drafter and verifier. For labs deploying frontier models, the zero-overhead path often wins in practice even if τ is lower on paper.</p>

<p>DFlash is the most radical departure: rather than predicting discrete tokens autoregressively, it treats the next K positions as a joint denoising problem. This unlocks very high throughput in batch settings — which is exactly why MiMo uses it to push past 1000 tokens/second — but requires the model architecture to support diffusion-based generation, not just a bolt-on module.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>2.76 vs 2.55 — A Free 8% Improvement</h2>
<p>GLM-5's parameter-shared MTP achieves an accept length of <strong>2.76</strong> versus DeepSeek-V3.2's <strong>2.55</strong>, measured on GLM's private prompt set with 4 speculative steps. That's an 8% improvement in accept length — which translates directly to throughput — with no change in model size, KV cache, or serving infrastructure.</p>

<p>The improvement is entirely due to the training-time coverage: by unrolling the same MTP weights at depths 1, 2, and 3, GLM-5 trains the draft head to be useful across all positions it will be asked to speculate at inference. DeepSeek-V3's single-depth training leaves tokens 3 and 4 under-optimized.</p>

