<p>The broader implication is that parameter sharing during MTP training is a <strong>Pareto improvement</strong> over DeepSeek-V3's approach: strictly better acceptance rate at the same memory cost. It doesn't require a separate drafter model, doesn't change the serving stack, and the implementation is a one-line change to MTP training — share the layer weights across unrolling steps.</p>

<p>As frontier models continue scaling to 700B+ parameters with MoE architectures, built-in speculative decoding becomes increasingly attractive. External drafters like EAGLE-3 require training a new drafter for each new base model (which happens every few months at frontier labs). MTP's parameter sharing is set-and-forget: train once, ship with the model, speculate for free.</p>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly hidden summary -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display:none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: MTP with Parameter Sharing in GLM-5</h2>
    <p>Speculative decoding speeds up LLM inference by letting a cheap draft model guess multiple tokens at once, then verifying them in a single forward pass through the big model. The key metric is accept length tau — how many draft tokens get accepted per verification step.</p>
    <p>There are three main approaches to drafting. EAGLE-3 trains a separate neural network on the target model's hidden states, achieving tau around 6.6 but requiring a custom drafter per base model. DFlash uses block diffusion to generate token candidates jointly, enabling over 1000 tokens per second at scale. Multi-Token Prediction, or MTP, bakes draft heads directly into the base model during pretraining — no extra serving infrastructure needed.</p>
    <p>MTP's challenge is that N speculative steps traditionally require N separate transformer layers, making memory grow linearly with speculation depth. DeepSeek-V3 worked around this by training just one MTP layer but using it at inference to speculate multiple positions — causing a training-inference mismatch that hurts acceptance on tokens 3 and 4.</p>
    <p>GLM-5 solves this with parameter sharing: train three MTP layers that all share the same weights. The model unrolls the same weights at depths 1, 2, and 3 during training, so the shared weights learn to be useful across all prediction distances. Memory stays flat at one layer's worth of weights, but acceptance improves because the weights have seen every depth during training. The result: an accept length of 2.76 versus DeepSeek-V3.2's 2.55 — an 8% improvement with zero extra deployment cost.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：GLM-5 的 MTP 参数共享技术</h2>
    <p>推测性解码通过让小型草稿模型一次猜测多个词元来加速大语言模型推理，然后通过大模型的单次前向传播验证所有词元。关键指标是接受长度 τ——每次验证步骤平均接受多少个草稿词元。</p>
    <p>主要有三种草稿生成方法。EAGLE-3 在目标模型的隐藏状态上训练独立的神经网络，τ 约为 6.6，但需要为每个基础模型单独训练草稿器。DFlash 使用块扩散联合生成候选词元，可实现超过每秒 1000 个词元的吞吐量。多词元预测（MTP）在预训练期间直接将草稿头集成到基础模型中，无需额外的服务基础设施。</p>
    <p>MTP 的挑战在于 N 个推测步骤传统上需要 N 个独立的 Transformer 层，导致内存随推测深度线性增长。DeepSeek-V3 的解决方案是只训练一个 MTP 层，但在推理时将其用于多个位置——这造成了训练推理不匹配，降低了第三和第四个词元的接受率。</p>
    <p>GLM-5 通过参数共享解决了这个问题：训练三个共享相同权重的 MTP 层。在训练时，相同的权重在深度 1、2、3 上分别展开，使共享权重学会在所有预测距离上都有效。内存保持在单层权重的水平，但由于权重在训练时经历了每个深度，接受率得到提升。结果：接受长度达到 2.76，优于 DeepSeek-V3.2 的 2.55——在零额外部署成本下实现了 8% 的提升。</p>
  </div>
</div>

